    "new_index_insertions": 4,
    "proved": "The restricted unit-divisor product locus admits arbitrary smooth admissible curve backgrounds, including zero areas, with a complete geodesic sum polar metric, ideal analytic closure and unique nearest-point ideal retraction. Its diameter and bounded Hausdorff comparison are controlled exactly by curve areas in the ordered case. Area-decreasing maps are surjective ideal-commuting contractions with explicit lost-coordinate fiber diameters. Uniform zero-area degeneration converges in L1 to the singleton retaining the normalized smooth curve potentials.",
    "mechanism": "Separated curve projections and countable-divisor domination give the base model identities through every nonnegative auxiliary coefficient; unit-divisor reduction transfers them upstairs. Closed positive test forms prove nonbigness at zero auxiliary coefficient or any zero curve area. Curve polar classification and the weighted metric comparison transfer all metric statements. Actual rational atomic masses prove analytic density, while irrational area ratios need not preserve individual analytic models. Uniform curve zero-area collapse and fiber integration prove the product limit.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only the separated unit-divisor locus is metrized; the top-volume comparison expression is identically zero there. No extension to unrestricted ordinary models or nonseparated obstacle formula is claimed. Zero-area smooth gauge potentials remain in the actual limiting potential."
  },
